  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nAn expansive surface ridge sits east of the area today and low \npressure associated with a mid-level shortwave trough is over South \nDakota. Strengthening southerly flow and sunshine will take \ntemperatures into the upper 80s this afternoon. Following a dry \nforecast today, a slight chance for storms returns late tonight and \nearly Saturday as a cold front associated with the low over SD moves \ninto northern KS. Confidence remains low on coverage of rain with a \ncontinued lack of concensus from models. The higher resolution short-\nterm models do not show much in the way of development, while the \ncoarser long-range deterministic models are still showing higher \ncoverage of early morning rain. If storms develop, severe weather is \nnot expected tonight and early Saturday. However, a better chance for\nsevere storms will occur Saturday afternoon and evening near the \nboundary. Trends from models have been to take the boundary further \nsouth throughout the day, so by 00Z Sunday it could be near or just \nsouth of I-35. HREF has up to 3000 J/kg of SBCAPE near and south of \nthe front, but depending on which model is analyzed, MLCAPE could \napproach 4000 J/kg without any lingering rain/cloud cover from the \nA.M. Deep layer shear of 40 to 50 kts and high DCAPE will support a \nlarge hail (up to 2\" inches in diameter) and damaging wind (60 to 70 \nmph) risk with any convection that organizes late in the day. A \nnocturnal LLJ and some isentropic ascent over the boundary could \nallow for additional development Saturday night. The chance for \nsevere storms, however, will wane overnight as instability diminishes\nand shear weakens.\n\nThe surface boundary will slowly start to move back northward on \nSunday while a mid-level ridge expands over the area from the south.\nStorms could again focus near the boundary, with the better chances \nin far northeast KS by the afternoon. Lingering A.M. cloud cover \ncould help limit instability, but if a few storms get going in \nnortheast KS during the afternoon, a few organized updrafts could \noccur. The warm front will then quickly surge northward on Monday as\nsoutherly flow increases through the southern and central Plains. A \ncap and lack of forcing should keep conditions dry.\n\nThe surface front returns yet again on Tuesday as low pressure moves \ninto southwest KS, and eventually toward the OK/TX panhandles. The \nexact position of the boundary through the area is a bit uncertain \nstill, but it could act as another focus for convection. The NBM \nkeeps POPs high in the 40 to 80 percent range Tuesday and Wednesday \nmorning. This may be a bit overdone, at least in timescale, since \nvarious long range models have different ideas of frontal position \nand associated areas of rain. Without good confidence on where the \nfront will be, determining severe risk is challenging at this range.\n\nMonday looks to be the warmest day of this forecast package with\nhighs approaching the mid 90s. Heat index values could be in the 100\nto 103 degree range. Otherwise, highs will generally range from the\nupper 70s north of the front to the upper 80s south of the front each\nday.",
